#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Out-of-process liveness watchdog for the whiskeyjack worker.

Why this exists, and why it is NOT part of the worker: an in-process notifier cannot
report its own death. `notify.py` fires when the code notices something; it says nothing
at all when the timer is disabled, the activation is retired, the user session ends, or
the process never starts. That is the 2026-09-09 outage -- 2h33m, ~28 refused polls,
and the only page was systemd's OnFailure with a body of `result=exit-code exit=1`.
The worker's own `poll_summary` digest is throttled to 24h, so as an AFK monitor it is
useless: it reports a stop up to a day late.

This runs as a SEPARATE systemd timer and reads the ledger READ-ONLY. It touches no
AppConfig field, so it cannot change `config_sha256` and cannot retire an activation
(M1-334).

The detector is heartbeat staleness. Measured against the real outage: heartbeats
stopped completely for a clean 160-minute gap (2026-09-09T23:00:19Z ->
2026-09-10T01:40:15Z), so this signal does catch the failure that actually happened.

Long forecasting runs are NOT false alarms: a poll can legitimately run for tens of
minutes (the unit allows 2400s), and systemd will not start a second instance while one
is running, so the heartbeat goes stale while the worker is healthy. Staleness is
therefore only an alert when the service is not currently active.

Two subjects, deliberately kept apart (M1-341). The second is the six-hourly resolution
ingestion and scoring schedule, `whiskeyjack-resolutions.timer` (M4-805). That unit has an
`OnFailure=` pager, and a pager reports a run that FAILS -- while a timer that is disabled
or stopped never runs, so it never fails and nothing says a word. Ingestion and scoring
just stop. It gets no heartbeat rule: the service writes no heartbeat and runs every six
hours, so the only honest signals are whether the timer is enabled and active and whether
the last run failed.

The two subjects keep separate state, separate throttles and separate pushes. Folding the
second into the first would make either fault mute the other's page through one shared
`last_alert`, and would send "worker recovered" while the other half was still stopped --
the failure `docs/M4-NOTES.md` (M4-805) rejected once already, in the other direction.

A third subject (M1-347): MiniBench rolling over to a Metaculus project the live activation
does not cover. On 2026-09-21 00:00 UTC the series moved from project 33122 to 33125, and for
about twelve hours the worker polled the old project, exited 0 with `discovered: 0` and kept its
heartbeat fresh -- healthy by every signal above, and 13 questions lost. So this asks Metaculus
what the `minibench` slug currently resolves to and compares it with the project the latest
activation is bound to. It is the only subject that touches the network for its evidence, and a
failed read is its own fault (`rollover_check_failed`) with its own throttle: never a green, and
never a "worker down" -- a Metaculus outage is not the worker's.
"""

